{"author":{"id":"7674679dac9170669f752d932f8b5955b1d55717359e13b1c1e687003c7bb99f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794175036,"nonce":"F2h08px5ONJQ7h+BszRt8vGBfUw=","message":"Follow-up to the commit above, by the user's direction: **the sync never stops on copies the rule cannot order.** `NewIndex` erroring there stopped the whole run, against `doc/design/jira.md` \"Failure classes\" (an issue's problem is reported and the run goes on).\n\n- **`NewIndex` no longer fails.** It groups the copies of each `jira-id` and calls `FirstReached` once over all of them (not pairwise). On an `ErrMultipleMatch` the Jira id is recorded as unresolved (`Index.Unresolved`) and given no winner.\n- **The run reports it once, `skipped`**, on the Jira key, with a reason naming every copy: \"2 local copies of one Jira issue (\u003ca\u003e, \u003cb\u003e) and no way to order them; archive all but one\". Nothing of it moves that run. Scan puts neither copy in linked or losers. Its search hit is not imported as a third copy. A link request or an in-doubt create naming it is skipped with the same reason, and a relation to it waits with that reason. Everything else syncs. Exit status follows the existing rule for skipped.\n- **No fallback to the lower id.** Alias lookup refuses to choose there, and the sync agrees with it.\n- **Resolution: archive every copy but one.** The rule ignores archive wherever there is a contact order. It now counts only where nothing else orders: when no copy reached the system, the one unarchived copy is first, in `cache.firstOf`. So the sync and alias resolution still agree. The next run syncs that copy as linked, and its first marker is then a contact, so the immutable order holds from then on. Not `issue rm`: it is local only, and the copy comes back on the next pull.\n- **Docs.** New heading `doc/design/cli.md` \"Archiving breaks a tie nothing else orders\". New heading `doc/design/jira.md` \"Copies the rule cannot order are skipped\". The Consolidation section is qualified where it says the archived flag is not an input, and it says a copy *the sync* linked has a contact. \"Failure classes\" now names this case as an issue's problem.\n- **Tests.** `jira` `TestUnorderableCopiesAreSkipped`: two copies, no contacts, plus a normal issue. The sync completes, the normal issue is created, there is one skipped line naming both ids, and neither copy's ref is touched. After archiving one copy, the next run syncs the other and the key names it. `cache` `TestAliasArchiveBreaksATie`.","files":null}]}